<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rustaq</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rustaq is a town in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> on the inland side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajar-mountains">Hajar Mountains</a>, dominated by a large fort. In 1624 Ibadi scholars there elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasir-ibn-murshid">Nasir ibn Murshid</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a>, founding the Ya'aruba dynasty, and the town served at several times as a seat of the imams and of the early Al Bu Said rulers. It is known for its hot springs and falaj irrigation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rustaq.jpg" alt="Rustaq" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hans Birger Nilsen · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al_Rustaq_Fort_(2)_(27255509348).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Rustaq is a town and district in the Al Batinah South Governorate of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a>, in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajar-mountains">Hajar Mountains</a>. The mountains surround it on every side but the north, where it opens onto the Batinah coastal plain towards Al-Musannah and Al-Suwayq. That northern side is a vital passage, and it links the town to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a>, about 160 km away. To the north-east the surroundings are rocky and sandy desert. The name comes from a Middle Iranian word, rōstāg, meaning a district, and a related New Iranian word, rusta, means a large village.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the days of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>, Rustaq had a Persian marzban, or military governor, to whom the Sasanians in the area reported. Dibba and Tuwwam, places that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> today shares with the U.A.E., were taxed by the Al-Julanda, who were clients of the Sasanians.</p><p>The town was once the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> in the time of Imam Nasir bin Murshid al-Ya'arubi. Its great fort, Rustaq Castle, stands on the slopes of the Green Mountain at the edge of the Batinah plain. The present structure was rebuilt by the first imams of the Ya'aruba dynasty between 1624 and 1649. It has two main floors above the ground floor, with residences, storerooms for weapons, reception rooms, a mosque, prisons and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a>, four gates and four towers.</p><p>One tower, the Wind Tower, was built by Imam <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saif-bin-sultan">Saif bin Sultan</a> al-Ya'arubi, whose tomb is in the western corner of the castle, and another, the Modern Tower, was added in the reign of Imam Ahmed bin Said. Nearby Al-Hazm Fort was built in 1711 by Imam <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> bin Saif bin Sultan al-Ya'arubi and is noted for its thick walls and its roof built without wooden supports.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Rustaq is known for its hot springs, such as Ain al-Kasfah, whose sulphurous water runs at about 45 degrees Celsius and is regarded as a cure for rheumatism and skin diseases. Falaj Al-Meisar, one of the traditional irrigation channels at Al-Khubayb in the district, was added to the World Heritage List in 2006 with four other aflaj. Date palms, citrus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beekeeping">beekeeping</a>, Omani halwa and the making of the khanjar dagger are among the local products.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rustaq” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1624 Ibadi scholars at <b>Rustaq</b> elected Nasir ibn Murshid al-Ya'rubi as imam, ending the rule of the Nabhani dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/yaaruba-dynasty">Ya'aruba dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
